= Cell membrane — Technical =

== Structure and permeability ==
The amphipathic lipid bilayer creates a hydrophobic interior, reducing spontaneous permeability to charged solutes and many polar molecules. Integral and peripheral proteins determine selective transport, adhesion, detection and localized biochemical reactions. Lipid composition, asymmetry, crowding and the cytoskeleton alter membrane properties.

== Passive and active transport ==
Channel proteins can conduct specific ions or molecules down electrochemical gradients; carrier proteins may transport solutes passively or couple movement to an energy source or another gradient. Pump activity can maintain concentration and voltage differences across the membrane. A flux model must specify species, concentration, membrane potential, transport kinetics, area and temperature.

== Model and measurement limitations ==
Membrane potential is an electrical difference, not proof that all ionic species are individually at equilibrium. A constant concentration can result from balanced, energy-dependent flux. Experimental methods such as electrophysiology, fluorescence imaging or tracer assays observe different aspects and need calibration and controls.

